A 37-year-old woman presents to her primary care physician with a 6-month history of fatigue. She denies any recent history of fevers, chills or headaches. She does not smoke or drink alcohol. A CBC demonstrates a microcytic anemia. Iron studies are ordered and demonstrate the following:
Serum iron: 40 µg/dL (normal 50–170 µg/dL);
TIBC: 230 µg/dL (normal 250–370 µg/dL);
Transferrin saturation: 10% (normal 15–50%);
Serum ferritin: 180 µg/L (normal 15-150 µg/L);
Which of the following is a likely cause of this patient's iron studies findings?

A 34-year-old woman with no chronic medical conditions presents to the emergency department after three days of high-grade fever, rapidly progressive dyspnoea and a non-productive cough. She returned from a six-week work assignment in the Republic of the Congo two months ago and completed weekly mefloquine prophylaxis, taking her final dose three weeks earlier. She has never smoked (including e-cigarettes), takes no regular medications and reports no recent chemical or animal exposures. Vital signs are: temperature 39 °C, heart rate 108 bpm, respiratory rate 30 breaths /min, blood pressure 110/68 mm Hg and SpO2 88 % on high-flow oxygen. Lung auscultation reveals diffuse crackles. A portable chest radiograph is obtained (Figure 1).

[Aetiologic Clarification] Further history is obtained to identify a potential trigger. The patient:
• completed four additional weekly doses of mefloquine after returning home (last dose three weeks ago);
• received a one-week course of minocycline for acne five months ago and has taken no tetracyclines since;
• received an inactivated influenza vaccine one week ago;
• took nitrofurantoin six months ago for cystitis;
• denies cigarette or e-cigarette use, illicit drug use, new pets, hot-tub exposure or recent renovation work.
Respiratory viral PCR panel on admission is negative. Which of the following is the MOST likely precipitating factor for her current lung disease?
